.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt KLDXREF 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldxref
.Nd generate hints for the kernel loader
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Rdv
.Op Fl f Ar hintsfile
.Ar path ...
.Nm
.Fl m Ar module
.Nm
.Fl o Ar output
.Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility is used to generate hint files which list modules, their
version numbers, and the files that contain them.
These hints are used by the kernel loader to determine where to find a
particular KLD module.
.Pp
A separate hint file is generated for each directory listed on the
command line that contains modules.
If no hint records are generated for a particular directory, no hint
file is created, and the preexisting hint file (if there was one in
that directory) is removed.
.Pp
.Nm
only processes files that either have no dots in their name like
.Pa kernel
or that end in
.Dq .ko
like
.Pa foo.ko .
.Pp
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl R
Recurse into subdirectories.
.It Fl d
Do not generate a hint file, but print module metadata on standard
output.
.It Fl f Ar hintsfile
Specify a different name for the hints files than
.Pa linker.hints .
.It Fl m Ar module
Print information about the modules in the file
.Ar module .
.It Fl o Ar output
Generate one hints file from only the explicitly named regular files.
Do not discover additional files or traverse directories.
All input and output paths must be canonical absolute paths in the same
existing directory, since the hints record each input's basename.
Symbolic links, non-regular inputs, duplicate inputs and using the output as
an input are rejected.
At least one input is required.
An existing output must be a regular file.
.Pp
Inputs are processed in argument order and must have compatible ELF
architectures even when some contain no metadata records.
If none of the selected objects has records, write a version-only index in
the selected objects' byte order.
The output is written to a temporary file and closed before replacement;
a failed invocation preserves the existing output.
This mode cannot be combined with directory or display options.
It selects inputs but does not authenticate or approve them.
.It Fl v
Operate in verbose mode.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
To build hint files for both standard and add-on modules:
.Pp
.Dl "kldxref /boot/kernel /boot/modules"
.Pp
To build hint files for all installed kernels:
.Pp
.Dl "kldxref -R /boot"
.Pp
To index only module files selected by a verified construction inventory:
.Pp
.Dl "kldxref -o /candidate/boot/modules/linker.hints /candidate/boot/modules/driver.ko"
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Failure to read selected module metadata, traverse an input directory, write or
close a hints file, install it by rename, or remove obsolete hints returns a
nonzero status.
An explicitly selected malformed module also returns failure.
Errors returned through the scan and output paths remove the current
directory's temporary hints file and preserve its previous hints file.
Fatal parser exits also discard the unfinished temporary hints file.
A successfully scanned directory with no records still removes obsolete hints.
A valid relocatable ELF object without a module-metadata section contributes no
records and is not an error.
Malformed PNP descriptions, invalid table dimensions, fields extending beyond
an entry, and failed PNP string reads cause failure.
.Pp
Directories are installed separately.
An error in a later directory does not undo hints already installed for earlier
directories; this is not a transaction over the entire invocation.
Callers constructing a protected system generation must reject the candidate
on any failure.
Concurrent modification of inputs or destination directories is unsupported.
The directory filename selection rules are not an authenticated module
inventory.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kld 4 ,
.Xr kldconfig 8 ,
.Xr kldload 8 ,
.Xr kldstat 8 ,
.Xr kldunload 8
